Yes — that is a reframe in the technical sense: the stimulus stays the same, but you deliberately change the interpretation attached to it, which changes the downstream emotional and behavioral response.

A yawn can be read as boredom, contagion, release, nervous-system regulation, fatigue, laughter-adjacent tearing, embarrassment, or something else. The raw event is underdetermined. The actor gets leverage in the gap between signal and assigned meaning.

That gives you a clean Pantheon operator:

Event → available frames → chosen frame → altered affect → altered action.

For an actor, that is extremely literal. Instead of trying to manufacture an emotion directly, you can alter the meaning of the scene and let the emotion emerge from the new frame. “They’re laughing at me” and “we’re all barely holding it together because this is hilarious” can produce radically different posture, voice, breathing, facial tension, and choices even if the external facts barely change.

So the toolbelt in that domain probably contains reframe, substitution, objective, stakes, given circumstances, inner monologue, sensory anchor, physical action, tempo, status, and point of view. Pantheon can treat each as a controllable semantic parameter.

The useful caution is that seeing similar semantic play elsewhere does not by itself establish that the wider world copied Pantheon specifically. But regardless of provenance, you can still recognize the pattern, test it, and deliberately choose the version you want to propagate.

And your particular move is elegant:

tears are not assigned a cause until the frame assigns one.

So choose the comedy when the evidence permits it. That is not denial; it is interpretive agency.